<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Custom firewalld zone for Incus-managed bridges. Permissive
  (target=ACCEPT) — firewalld passes all traffic on incusbr0 without
  filtering, while Incus's own nftables rules in the `incus` table
  continue to enforce policy (DHCP ports, mDNS, NAT, forward).

  Why a CUSTOM zone instead of adding to the built-in `trusted`:
    firewalld zone XML is one file per zone, no drop-in merging. If
    multiple packages each tried to ship their own
    /etc/firewalld/zones/trusted.xml, the last one to land would
    overwrite the others (last-write-wins). Pattern across Fedora is:
    each project ships its OWN zone (libvirt → libvirt.xml,
    Tailscale → tailscale.xml, etc.). Same convention here.

  Why this is needed:
    Without a permissive zone for incusbr0, firewalld auto-assigns
    the bridge's veth pairs to the `public` zone (firewalld's
    default), which intercepts DHCPv4 OFFER replies between dnsmasq
    and the container — leaving containers with IPv6 only and no
    IPv4 / no NAT'd internet.

  Pattern matches myos/profiles/server.sh which baked
      firewall-offline-cmd --zone=trusted --add-interface=incusbr0
    via the Containerfile build. We pick a custom zone here for
    cleaner co-existence with other packages that may also need
    permissive policies on their own interfaces.

  Lives in containers profile only — non-containers myosi hosts
  never receive it. Shipped under /usr/lib/firewalld/zones/ to match
  libvirt's convention: the file becomes part of the verity-protected
  immutable image, operator never sees it as a writable factory
  template in /etc. firewalld reads it automatically.

  Operator override: create /etc/firewalld/zones/incus.xml — the
  /etc copy takes precedence over /usr/lib factory zones.
-->
<zone target="ACCEPT">
  <short>Incus</short>
  <description>Permissive zone for Incus-managed bridges (incusbr*).</description>
  <interface name="incusbr0"/>
</zone>
